The core–shell ZIF-8@ZIF-67 particles are then subjected to thermal annealing in flowing \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) at \(800\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) at a ramp rate of \(2\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\ \mathrm{min}^{-1}\), followed by etching away the unstable Co nanoparticles with \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}\). It has been found that well-defined hollow nanocages instead of solid nanoparticles commonly reported in literature up to now are created by the decomposition and carbonization of ZIF-8@ZIF-67 crystals.[31] The FESEM analysis shows that the as-synthesized samples have inherited the polyhedral shape of the parent ZIF-8@ZIF-67 crystals without collapse (Figure 2a,b). The average edge length of the particles is slightly reduced to \(\approx 500{-}520\ \mathrm{nm}\) due to the shrinkage to denser carbon-based composites. The particles have a rough surface anchored with numerous short carbon nanotubes (CNTs), as shown in Figure 2c. The formation of CNTs is the result of the catalytic effect of Co nanoparticles on the particle surface.[11] A FESEM image of a broken particle clearly indicates the presence of inner cavities underneath the carbon shells (Figure 2c). The structure of closely packed double shells with uniform thickness is visualized by TEM characterization (Figure 2d,e). The ZIF-8-derived inner shells are composed of dense amorphous carbon with an average thickness of \(\approx 60\ \mathrm{nm}\), which are formed by the decomposition of ZIF-8 to \(\mathrm{ZnO}\)/carbon composite and subsequent carbothermal reduction to \(\mathrm{Zn}\)/carbon composite.

