The structural characteristics of \(\mathrm{NC}@\mathrm{Co-NGC}\) DSNCs are identified by XRD analysis (Figure 3a). The relatively sharp peak at \(26^{\circ}\) corresponds to (002) plane of graphitic carbon while the others at \({\approx}44^{\circ}\) , \(51^{\circ}\) , and \(76^{\circ}\) are attributed to the diffraction from (111), (200), and (220) planes of metallic Co \((a=3.545\mathrm{~\AA~}\) , JCPDS No. 15-0806). Figure S5 (Supporting Information) shows the Raman spectra of \(\mathrm{NC}@\mathrm{Co-NGC}\) DSNCs, ZIF-67 derived Co-NGC and ZIF-8 derived NC. For \(\mathrm{NC}@\mathrm{Co-NGC}\) and Co-NGC, the G bands at around \(1589~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) corresponds to the in-plane vibration of \(\mathrm{sp}^{2}\) carbon while the D band at around \(1338~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) mainly reflects the structural defects in graphitic structure. The intensity ratio between them \(\left(I_{\mathrm{D}}/I_{\mathrm{G}}\right)\) is \({\approx}0.96\) , revealing the domination of short-range order graphitic structure. Compared to NC, the G band of \(\mathrm{NC}@\mathrm{Co-NGC}\) and Co-NGC is

upshifted by \(12~\,\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) in wavelength, suggesting strong electronic interaction in them, possibly between carbon and metal atoms. Thermogravimetric analysis (TGA) reveals that the content of metallic Co in \(\mathrm{NC}@\mathrm{Co-NGC}\) DSNCs is \(16.8\,\mathrm{\wt}\%\) (Figure S6, Supporting Information). The chemical composition and the effect of N doping are investigated by X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS), revealing the presence of C, N, O, and Co elements in \(\mathrm{NC}@\mathrm{Co-NGC}\) DSNCs, where the N content is calculated to be \(5.28\;\mathrm{at}\%\) (Figure 3b). The high-resolution N 1s spectrum reveals the presence of two types of N species, namely, the pyridinic \(\mathrm{N}\approx\!398.4\ \mathrm{eV}\) and graphitic N \((\approx\!400.9\;\mathrm{eV})\) in the nanocages (Figure 3c). The Co 2p XPS spectrum is resolved into two pairs of \(2{\tt P}_{3/2}/2{\tt P}_{1/2}\) doublets for metallic Co \((778.0/792.6\,\mathrm{eV})\) and N-coordinated \(\mathrm{Co}^{2+}\left(\mathrm{Co}{-}\mathrm{N}_{\mathrm{x}}\right)\) (780.7/795.3 eV) with an energy separation of \(14.6\ \mathrm{\eV}\) (Figure 3d).[33,34] The C1s XPS spectrum can be deconvoluted into the peaks for \(\mathrm{sp}^{2}\) -hydridized carbon \((284.6~\mathrm{eV})\) , \(\mathrm{C}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{O}\) bonds \((286.5\,\mathrm{\eV})\) and \(\mathrm{O-C=O}\) group \((288.8~\mathrm{eV})\) , as shown in Figure S7a (Supporting Information).[35] The identification of \(\mathrm{C}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{N}\) bonds at \(285.1~\mathrm{eV}\) confirms the N doping in \(\mathrm{NC}@\mathrm{Co-NGC}\) DSNCs.[35] The presence of O signal can be attributed to residual oxygen in ZIFderived carbon and the adsorbed oxygen from environmental atmosphere (Figure S7b, Supporting Information). The \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) adsorption/desorption isotherms of \(\mathrm{NC}@\mathrm{Co-NGC}\) DSNCs are type-I isotherms (Figure 3e), in which the isotherms with sharp increase in volume at low relative pressure indicate the presence of micropores, mainly from ZIF-8-derived NC shells. The hysteresis loop at relative pressure between 0.5 and 0.9 suggests the coexistence of mesopores from Co-decorated graphitic carbon shell derived from ZIF-67. The pore size distribution indicates that the micropores are smaller than \(1\,\textrm{n m}\) in size, while the mesopores are below \(70\ \mathrm{nm}\) (Figure 3f). The as-synthesized \(\mathrm{NC}@\mathrm{Co-NGC}\) DSNCs with unique hollow structure
and hierarchical porosity have a Brunauer–Emmett–Teller surface area of \(\approx 476\;\mathrm{m}^{2}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) and total pore volume of \(0.303\;\mathrm{m}^{3}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\).

The formation of double-shelled \(\mathrm{NC}@\mathrm{Co}-\mathrm{NGC}\) nanocages is believed to be governed by a surface-stabilized contraction process originated from the thermal stability difference of ZIF-67 and ZIF-8. TGA analysis reveals that ZIF-67 is less stable than ZIF-8 (Figure S8, Supporting Information), and would decompose first to form rigid shells of \(\mathrm{CoO/carbon}\) composite around ZIF-8 cores at above \(500\;^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) (Figures S9a,e and S10, Supporting Information). Then, the rigid \(\mathrm{CoO/carbon}\) shells could generate an outward adhesive force at the interface between them and ZIF-8 cores, which suppress the inward contraction of ZIF-8 due to the thermally induced polycondensation. As a result, hollow cavities are formed inside ZIF-8 cores as a balance of the mass loss (mainly the small molecules such as \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) and \((\mathrm{CN})_{2}\)) from ZIF-8 decomposition at evaluated temperature (e.g., \(550\;^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\)) (Figure S9b,f).[16] At higher temperature (e.g., \(600{-}700\;^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\)), the \(\mathrm{CoO}\) in outer shells is reduced to metallic \(\mathrm{Co}\) by surrounding carbonaceous species formed in situ to catalyze the formation of graphitic carbon (Figures S9c,g and S10, Supporting Information). Eventually, double-shelled \(\mathrm{NC}@\mathrm{Co}-\mathrm{NGC}\) nanocages are obtained after the evaporation of nanosized \(\mathrm{Zn}\) at \(700{-}800\;^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) (Figure 3a; Figures S9d,h and S10, Supporting Information). Without stabilization by ZIF-67 shells, only solid carbon particles with shrunken volume would be formed due to the inward contraction of ZIF-8 under identical conditions. In this case, the vacancies generated by mass loss are largely squeezed out of the particles by contraction-induced stress (Figure S11, Supporting Information). To confirm the

surface-stabilized contraction mechanism proposed, coreshelled ZIF-67@ZIF-8 polyhedrons are fabricated and annealed at \(800\;^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\). As expected, only solid \(\mathrm{Co}-\mathrm{NGC}@\mathrm{NC}\) nanoparticles instead of hollow structure are yielded in this case because of the lower stability of ZIF-67 cores than that of ZIF-8 shells (Figure S12, Supporting Information). It should be noted that the reported nonequilibrium heterogeneous contraction is not applied for the formation of \(\mathrm{NC}@\mathrm{Co}-\mathrm{NGC}\) nanocages because a slow ramp rate of \(2\;^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\;\mathrm{min}^{-1}\) is unlikely to reach a sharp temperature gradient within the particles in this process.[37]

The electrocatalytic activity of \(\mathrm{NC}@\mathrm{Co}-\mathrm{NGC}\) DSNCs toward ORR was first identified by cyclic voltammogram (CV) tests conducted in Ar or \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\)-saturated \(0.1\;\mathrm{M}\;\mathrm{KOH}\) electrolyte at a scan rate of \(10\;\mathrm{mV}\;\mathrm{s}^{-1}\) (Figure 4a). A substantial reduction process is found to occur at \(\approx 0.81\;\mathrm{V}\) versus reversible hydrogen electrode in \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\)-saturated \(0.1\;\mathrm{M}\;\mathrm{KOH}\) solution, whereas featureless quasi-rectangular voltammogram is observed within the same voltage window in Ar-saturated electrolyte. To gain deeper insight into electrocatalytic activity and kinetics of \(\mathrm{NC}@\mathrm{Co}-\mathrm{NGC}\) DSNCs toward ORR, linear sweep voltammograms (LSVs) are recorded on a rotating disk electrode at a rotation rate of \(1600\;\mathrm{rpm}\) in \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\)-saturated \(0.1\;\mathrm{M}\;\mathrm{KOH}\) electrolyte at a scan rate of \(10\;\mathrm{mV}\;\mathrm{s}^{-1}\) (Figure 4b). The curves of \(\mathrm{NC}@\mathrm{Co}-\mathrm{NGC}\) DSNC electrodes show obvious plateau with an onset potential of \(\approx 0.92\;\mathrm{V}\) and a half-wave potential \((E_{1/2})\) of \(0.82\;\mathrm{V}\), which is comparable to those of \(\mathrm{Pt}/\mathrm{C}\) \((0.96\;\mathrm{V}, E_{1/2} = 0.82\;\mathrm{V})\), and outperform most previously reported carbon-based ORR catalysts (Table S1, Supporting Information). Moreover, the unique and wide current plateau from \(0.7\) to \(0\;\mathrm{V}\) observed in

