the case of the \(\mathsf{N C@C o}\) -NGC DSNC electrode represents a diffusion-controlled process corresponding to the efficient four-electron-dominated ORR pathway.[38] To demonstrate the advantage of \(\mathsf{N C@C o}\) -NGC DSNCs in oxygen electrocatalysis, the ORR performance of solid NC, Co-NGC, and \(\mathsf{C o-N G C@N C}\) particles is also investigated under identical conditions (Figures S13 and S14a, Supporting Information). In comparison with \(\mathsf{N C@C o}\) Co-NGC DSNCs, they exhibit higher onset overpotential \((0.33\,\mathrm{V}\) for Co-NGC, \(0.36\;\mathrm{V}\) for NC, \(0.34\;\mathrm{V}\) for \(\mathsf{C o-N G C@N C})\) and halfwave potential \((0.8\,\mathrm{~V~}\) for Co-NGC, \(0.73\mathrm{~V~}\) for NC, \(0.79\mathrm{~V~}\) for \(\mathsf{C o-N G C@N C})\) , as can be seen in Figure 4b and Figure S14a (Supporting Information). The superior catalytic activity of \(\mathsf{N C@C o}\) -NGC DSNCs toward ORR is further verified by the Tafel plots obtained from the polarization curves. The \(\mathsf{N C@C o}\) Co-NGC DSNC has a Tafel slope of \(53\ \mathrm{mV\dec^{-1}}\) , much lower than that of NC \((61\,\mathrm{mV\,dec^{-1}})\) , Co-NGC \((60\,\mathrm{mV\,dec^{-1}})\) , and even \(\mathrm{Pt}/\mathrm{C}\) \((57\,\mathrm{\mV\dec^{-1}})\) , suggesting the favorable ORR kinetics in the \(\mathsf{N C@C o}\) -NGC DSNCs electrocatalyst with a unique hollow nanostructure (Figure 4c). The apparent electrochemical surface area (ECSA) of \(\mathsf{N C@C o}\) -NGC, Co-NGC, and NC is compared by measuring their double-layer capacitance \((C_{\mathrm{dl}})\) , which is typically used to represent the ECSA (Figure S15, Supporting Information).[39–41] The \(\mathsf{N C@C o}\) -NGC possesses over \(60\%\) higher \(C_{\mathrm{dl}}\) than that of Co-NGC, revealing the positive effect of hollow nanostructure on electrocatalytic activity.

The electron transfer number per oxygen molecule (n) and kinetic limiting current density \(\left(J_{\mathrm{k}}\right)\) for ORR is determined from the LSV curves (Figure S16, Supporting Information) according to the Koutechy–Levich (K–L) equation. The K–L plots of \(\mathsf{N C@C o}\) -NGC DSNCs manifest good parallel linearity over the potential ranging from 0.71 to \(0.46\,\mathrm{~V},\) suggesting first-order reaction kinetics for ORR (Figure 4d).[42] It is noted that the \(\mathsf{N C@C o}\) -NGC DSNCs exhibit a similar slope with that of \(\mathrm{Pt}/\mathrm{C}\) electrodes. The \(n\) is determined to be \({\approx}4.0\) , indicating that the ORR proceeds via a four-electron-transfer scheme involving the in situ \(\mathrm{OH^{-}}\) formation in alkaline medium, which are further confirmed by the rotating ring-disk electrode measurements (Figure S16, Supporting Information).[43–45] The \(\mathsf{N C@C o}\) -NGC DSNC catalyst still shows an equal \(J_{\mathrm{k}}\) of \(12.8\;\mathrm{mA}\;\mathrm{cm}^{-2}\) to that of \(\mathrm{Pt}/\mathrm{C}\) , which is much higher than that of NC \((3.2\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}})\) and Co-NGC \((7.9\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}})\) catalyst (Figure S17, Supporting Information). In comparison with \(\mathrm{Pt}/\mathrm{C}\) catalyst, the \(\mathsf{N C@C o}\) -NGC DSNC catalyst exhibits superior durability and higher resistance to the methanol cross-over effect in oxygen-saturated \(0.1\mathrm{~M~}\) KOH (Figure S18, Supporting Information). Furthermore, the \(\mathsf{N C@C o}\) -NGC DSNC catalyst also shows excellent ORR activity in acidic medium. They exhibit much lower onset overpotential \((0.5\mathrm{~V})\) than that of Co-NGC \((0.57\mathrm{~V})\) and NC \((0.63\mathrm{~V})\) electrodes, as well as superior stability to commercial \(\mathrm{Pt}/\mathrm{C}\) catalysts (Figure S19, Supporting Information).

Co-NGC DSNCs manifest good parallel linearity over the potential ranging from 0.71 to \(0.46\,\mathrm{~V},\) suggesting first-order reaction kinetics for ORR (Figure 4d).[42] It is noted that the \(\mathsf{N C@C o}\) -NGC DSNCs exhibit a similar slope with that of \(\mathrm{Pt}/\mathrm{C}\) electrodes. The \(n\) .s determined to be \({\approx}4.0\) , indicating that the ORR proceeds via a four-electron-transfer scheme involving the in situ \(\mathrm{OH^{-}}\) formation in alkaline medium, which are further confirmed by the rotating ring-disk electrode measurements (Figure S16, Supporting Information).[43–45] The \(\mathsf{N C@C o}\) -NGC DSNC catalyst still shows an equal \(J_{\mathrm{{k}}}\) of \(12.8\;\mathrm{mA}\;\mathrm{cm}^{-2}\) to that of \(\mathrm{Pt}/\mathrm{C}\) , which is much higher than that of NC \((3.2\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}})\) and Co-NGC \((7.9\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}})\) catalyst (Figure S17, Supporting Information). In comparison with \(\mathrm{P}\mathrm{t}/\mathrm{C}\) catalyst, the \(\mathsf{N C@C o}\) -NGC DSNC catalyst exhibits superior durability and higher resistance to the methanol cross-over effect in oxygen-saturated \(0.1\mathrm{~M~}\) KOH (Figure S18, Supporting Information). Furthermore, the \(\mathsf{N C@C o}\) -NGC DSNC catalyst also shows excellent ORR activity in acidic medium. T hey exhibit much lower onset overpotential \((0.5\mathrm{~V})\) than that of Co-NGC \((0.57\mathrm{~V})\) and NC \((0.63\mathrm{~V})\) electrodes, as well as superior stability to commercial \(\mathrm{Pt}/\mathrm{C}\) catalysts (Figure S19, Supporting Information).

The oxygen evolution activities of \(\mathsf{N C@C o}\) -NGC DSNC catalysts are also evaluated in \(O_{2}\) -saturated \(0.1\mathrm{~M~KOH}\) solution with iR-correction. The \(\mathsf{N C@C o}\) -NGC DSNC catalyst reaches a current density of \(10\;\mathrm{mA}\;\mathrm{cm}^{-2}\) (a value expected for a \(10\%\) efficient solar water-splitting device) at a potential \(\left(E_{\mathrm{j}=10}\right)\) of \(1.64\,\mathrm{V},\) which is compares favorably to that of \(\mathrm{RuO}_{2}\) \((1.60\;\mathrm{V})\) , but much lower than those of Co-NGC \((1.70\mathrm{~V})\) , \(\mathsf{C o-N G C@N C}\) \((1.68\,\mathrm{~V})\) , \(\mathrm{Pt}/\mathrm{C}\) \((1.82\,\mathrm{~V})\) , and other reported catalysts (Figure 5a; Figure S14b
and Table S1, Supporting Information). Also, \(\mathrm{NC}@\mathrm{Co}-\mathrm{NGC}\) DSNC catalysts exhibit a much lower Tafel slope \((91\mathrm{~mV~dec}^{-1})\) with respect to NC \((115\mathrm{~mV~dec}^{-1})\), Co-NGC \((100\mathrm{~mV~dec}^{-1})\), \(\mathrm{RuO}_{2}\) \((110\mathrm{~mV~dec}^{-1})\), and \(\mathrm{Pt}/\mathrm{C}\) \((171\mathrm{~mV~dec}^{-1})\) (Figure 5b), suggesting the favorable OER kinetics in hollow nanostructured electrocatalyst. Much enhanced electrocatalytic activities are also observed for \(\mathrm{NC}@\mathrm{Co}-\mathrm{NGC}\) DSNC electrodes in \(1\,\mathrm{~M~}\) KOH electrolyte, where the onset potential is close to that of \(\mathrm{RuO}_{2}\) catalyst (Figure S20a, Supporting Information). The stability test results demonstrate that the \(\mathrm{NC}@\mathrm{Co}-\mathrm{NGC}\) DSNC catalyst is very stable for the OER, whereas \(\mathrm{RuO}_{2}\) exhibits a rapid voltage increase at the constant current of \(10\mathrm{~mA~cm}^{-2}\) in short time in \(0.1\mathrm{~M~}\) KOH (Figure S20b, Supporting Information).

The pyrolysis temperature is a critical factor in terms of tuning the activity of \(\mathrm{NC}@\mathrm{Co}-\mathrm{NGC}\) DSNC electrocatalysts due to its significant effect on the chemical composition and structure of the ZIF-derived products. As discussed above, the \(\mathrm{NC}@\mathrm{Co}-\mathrm{NGC}\) DSNC made at \(800\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) due to the formation of Co-NGC shells exhibits the highest current density and the lowest overpotential for ORR and OER (Figure S21, Supporting Information). Lower annealing temperature (e.g., \(600{-}700\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\)), however, yields the poor conductive composites made of carbon and Co nanoparticles of several nanometers as a result of inadequate graphitization process and carbonthermal reduction of metal oxides, accompanied with lower specific surface area \((359\,\mathrm{~m}^{2}\,\mathrm{~g}^{-1})\) (Figures S8, S22a–c, and S23, Supporting Information). While annealing at higher temperature (e.g., \(900\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\)) is not favorable for retaining high N-doping level in whole architecture and high specific surface area as a result of the destruction of micropores (Figures S22d,e and S23, Supporting Information). In this case, the Co particles with much larger size of tens to a hundred nanometer are yielded due to strong mobility and severe fusion of metal particles, which are less stable and can be easily removed by acidic etching (Figure S22f, Supporting Information). Meanwhile, the N content is also reduced to \(4\,\mathrm{at}\%\) by increasing the annealing temperature from 800 to \(900\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\), and the atomic ratio of pyridinic N in total N is decreased from \(70\%\) to \(45\%\) (Figure S24d, Supporting Information). The loss of N and stable Co in the hybrids significantly reduces the charge transfer and delocalization on carbon lattice and more importantly, weakens the interaction and electron transfer between carbon and metal atoms (to be further discussed below in detail), thereby being unfavorable for the chemisorption of reactive intermediates on large ECSA that help to accelerate the ORR and OER.[12,45] In the sample annealed at \(800\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\), the ratio of Co-NGC in \(\mathrm{NC}@\mathrm{Co}-\mathrm{NGC}\) DSNCs also affects their electrocatalytic performance. The \(\mathrm{NC}@\mathrm{Co}-\mathrm{NGC}\) DSNCs with \(16.8\,\mathrm{wt}\%\) Co show the lowest overpotential to the benchmark nanotage catalysts with lower (e.g., \(9.6\,\mathrm{wt}\%\) ) or higher (e.g., \(28.2\,\mathrm{wt}\%\) ) Co content towards ORR/OER because of more electrochemical active phase than the former but higher specific surface area than that of the latter (Figures S25–S27, Supporting Information).

The application potential of the \(\mathrm{NC}@\mathrm{Co}-\mathrm{NGC}\) DSNC materials as bifunctional oxygen electrocatalysts has been explored, and the overall oxygen electrode activity is evaluated by the difference of OER and ORR metrics \((\Delta E=E_{j-10}-E_{1/2})\). Remarkably, the \(\mathrm{NC}@\mathrm{Co}-\mathrm{NGC}\) DSNCs exhibit a \(\Delta E\) of \(0.82\,\mathrm{~V}\), much lower than that of \(\mathrm{Pt}/\mathrm{C}\) \((0.94\,\mathrm{~V})\), \(\mathrm{RuO}_{2}\) \((1.0\,\mathrm{~V})\), and Co-NGC

\((0.95\,\mathrm{~V})\), as well as most of the bifunctional catalysts reported to date such as metal-free carbon hybrids (e.g., N, S-carbon nanosheet, \(\Delta E=0.88\,\mathrm{~V}\)) and transition metal oxides (e.g., \(\mathrm{MnCo}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{4}/\mathrm{N}-\mathrm{CNT}\), \(\Delta E=1.02\,\mathrm{~V}\)) (Figure 5c; Table S1, Supporting Information).[38,46] Motivated by the superior catalytic activity of the \(\mathrm{NC}@\mathrm{Co}-\mathrm{NGC}\) DSNCs for both ORR and OER, the proof-to-concept tests were conducted to demonstrate the feasibility of \(\mathrm{NC}@\mathrm{Co}-\mathrm{NGC}\) DSNCs as the air cathode catalyst in primary Zn–air batteries (Figure S23a, Supporting Information). The open-circuit voltage of the Zn–air battery with \(\mathrm{NC}@\mathrm{Co}-\mathrm{NGC}\) DSNC electrode is \(1.45\,\mathrm{~V}\), which is comparable to the batteries with \(\mathrm{Pt}/\mathrm{C}\) electrode \((1.46\,\mathrm{~V})\) but higher than that of \(\mathrm{RuO}_{2}\) electrode \((1.31\,\mathrm{~V})\) (Figure S28b, Supporting Information). Three coin-type Zn–air batteries with \(\mathrm{NC}@\mathrm{Co}-\mathrm{NGC}\) DSNC catalysts can be connected in series to light a light-emitting diode (LED) array panel \((11\times44,3.7\,\mathrm{~V})\) (Figure S28c, Supporting Information). The batteries with \(\mathrm{NC}@\mathrm{Co}-\mathrm{NGC}\) DSNCs as a cathode catalyst exhibit high capacities of \(\approx617\), 602, 565, and \(537\,\mathrm{mA}\,\mathrm{h}\,\mathrm{g}_{\mathrm{Zn}}^{-1}\) at the current densities of 2, 5, 10, and \(20\,\mathrm{mA}\,\mathrm{cm}^{-2}\), respectively (Figure S28d, Supporting Information). These values are higher than that of the primary batteries with \(\mathrm{Pt}/\mathrm{C}\) catalyst, which deliver lower capacities of \(\approx580\), 535, 510, and \(495\,\mathrm{mA}\,\mathrm{h}\,\mathrm{g}_{\mathrm{Zn}}^{-1}\) at identical current densities (Figure S28e, Supporting Information). The peak power density of the Zn–air battery with \(\mathrm{NC}@\mathrm{Co}-\mathrm{NGC}\) DSNC electrode is \(109\,\mathrm{mW}\,\mathrm{cm}^{-2}\) (Figure S29, Supporting Information). The potential of \(\mathrm{NC}@\mathrm{Co}-\mathrm{NGC}\) DSNC catalyst in rechargeable Zn–air batteries is evaluated using identical cell configuration except add \(\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{Ac})_{2}\) \((0.2\,\mathrm{~M})\) into \(6\,\mathrm{~M~}\) KOH electrolyte (Figure S30a, Supporting Information). The charging and discharging polarization curves of rechargeable Zn–air batteries with \(\mathrm{NC}@\mathrm{Co}-\mathrm{NGC}\) DSNC, \(\mathrm{Pt}/\mathrm{C}\) or \(\mathrm{RuO}_{2}\) as cathode catalysts are shown in Figure S30b (Supporting Information). The \(\mathrm{NC}@\mathrm{Co}-\mathrm{NGC}\) DSNC catalyst exhibits comparable discharging performance to \(\mathrm{Pt}/\mathrm{C}\) catalyst at both low (e.g., \(20\,\mathrm{mA}\,\mathrm{cm}^{-2}\)) and high current densities (e.g., \(80\,\mathrm{mA}\,\mathrm{cm}^{-2}\)), but significantly outperforms \(\mathrm{RuO}_{2}\) catalysts in rechargeable Zn–air batteries. Upon charging, the \(\mathrm{NC}@\mathrm{Co}-\mathrm{NGC}\) DSNC catalyst shows substantially lower overpotential for driving OER than that of \(\mathrm{Pt}/\mathrm{C}\) and \(\mathrm{RuO}_{2}\) catalysts at the current densities of up to \(40\,\mathrm{mA}\,\mathrm{cm}^{-2}\), showing reliable bifunctionally electrocatalytic properties. The rechargeable Zn–air batteries with \(\mathrm{NC}@\mathrm{Co}-\mathrm{NGC}\) DSNC catalyst exhibit superior lifetime \((56\,\mathrm{h})\) and discharging voltage retention \((77.5\%)\) to that with \(\mathrm{Pt}/\mathrm{C}\) \((14\,\mathrm{h},50\%)\) or \(\mathrm{RuO}_{2}\) \((33\,\mathrm{h},59\%)\) catalyst upon discharging–charging at a current density of \(10\,\mathrm{mA}\,\mathrm{cm}^{-2}\), showing good reversibility and durability (Figure S30c–e, Supporting Information). This performance may be further enhanced by further optimizing the cell configuration, e.g., the three-electrode cells that OER and ORR electrocatalysts are loaded onto two separate electrodes for charge and discharge,[47,47] which can prevent the bifunctional catalyst from coming into contact with the oxidative (or reductive) potential during ORR (or OER).

Apparently, the Co-NGC shells in \(\mathrm{NC}@\mathrm{Co}-\mathrm{NGC}\) DSNCs contribute largely to the catalytic activities for both ORR and OER while the hollow nanostructure accelerates the reaction kinetics. To gain atomistic insight into the origin of bifunctional electrochemical activity, first-principles calculation in terms of density function theory (DFT) methods is performed to determine the active sites and catalytic reactions over Co-NGC shells. As
